        <body>
            <div xml:id="d806">

                <pb facs="1298" n="1202" sameAs="frus:frus1874/pg1202"/>

                <head>No. 803.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Beardsley to Mr. Fish.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Agency and Consulate-General of the United States in
                                Egytpt</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Cairo</hi></placeName>,
                            <date calendar="gregorian" when="1874-09-12"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >September</hi> 12, 1874</date>. (Received October 14.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 235.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: As everything relating to the expedition of
                    Colonel Gordon is <pb facs="1299" n="1203" xml:id="pg1203"/> of interest, I
                    inclose herewith certain papers taken from the London Mail.</p>
                <p>Inclosure No. 1 is a series of letters, comprising a correspondence between Sir
                    Samuel Baker and others in regard tothe slave-trade in Egypt, and the employment
                    of one Abou Saood by Colonel Gordon. This Abou Saood was mentioned in my
                    dispatch No. 123, of September 3, 1873, as being at that time in Cairo, and
                    under arrest.</p>
                <p>Inclosure No. 2 relates to the progress of the expedition.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c,</p>
                <closer><signed>R. BEARDSLEY.</signed></closer>











